{"name":"Silver Spruce","hex":"#cadfdd","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver spruce","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various spruce trees\nAn Engelmann spruce (Picea engelmannii)","An Engelmann spruce (Picea engelmannii)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_spruce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Picea engelmannii","blocks":[{"text":"Picea engelmannii, with the common names Engelmann spruce, mountain spruce, and silver spruce, is a species of spruce native to western North America. It is highly prized for producing distinctive tone wood for acoustic guitars and other instruments, it is mostly a high-elevation mountain tree but also appears in watered canyons."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Engelmann_spruce_on_the_PCT%2C_Washington.JPG/330px-Engelmann_spruce_on_the_PCT%2C_Washington.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picea_engelmannii","wikidata":"Q165387"},{"title":"Spruce","blocks":[{"text":"A spruce is a tree of the genus Picea, a genus of some 37 species of coniferous trees in the pine family, Pinaceae, found in the northern temperate and boreal (taiga) regions of the Northern Hemisphere. Picea is treated either in the subfamily Pinoideae, or the sole genus in its own subfamily Piceoideae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Picea_abies.jpg/330px-Picea_abies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spruce","wikidata":"Q26782"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"},{"title":"Silver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver or metallic gray is a color tone resembling gray that is a representation of the color of polished silver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png/330px-Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_(color)","wikidata":"Q317802"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Picea_engelmannii_levila.png/500px-Picea_engelmannii_levila.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Picea_engelmannii_levila.png/500px-Picea_engelmannii_levila.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APicea_engelmannii_levila.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Picea_engelmannii_Sierra_Blanca_NM.jpg/500px-Picea_engelmannii_Sierra_Blanca_NM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Picea_engelmannii_Sierra_Blanca_NM.jpg/1280px-Picea_engelmannii_Sierra_Blanca_NM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Picea_engelmannii_Sierra_Blanca_NM.jpg/500px-Picea_engelmannii_Sierra_Blanca_NM.jpg?utm_sour